```java
// Anti-pattern: every request opens a brand-new physical connection.
// Each getConnection() pays the full TCP handshake + authentication.
public List<Order> findOrders() throws SQLException {
    // ~50ms wasted here on EVERY call, before any query runs:
    try (Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, pass);
         PreparedStatement ps = con.prepareStatement("SELECT * FROM orders")) {
        // ... map ResultSet ...
        return orders;
    }
    // Connection is physically closed and destroyed when the block exits.
    // Under load this floods the DB with connect/disconnect churn.
}
```

```java
// Build the pool ONCE at startup (Spring Boot does this for you).
HikariConfig cfg = new HikariConfig();
cfg.setJdbcUrl(url);
cfg.setUsername(user);
cfg.setPassword(pass);
cfg.setMaximumPoolSize(10);            // cap total connections
DataSource pool = new HikariDataSource(cfg);

public List<Order> findOrders() throws SQLException {
    // borrow an already-open connection — no handshake, no auth round-trip:
    try (Connection con = pool.getConnection();
         PreparedStatement ps = con.prepareStatement("SELECT * FROM orders")) {
        // ... map ResultSet ...
        return orders;
    }
    // close() RETURNS the connection to the pool — it is reused, not destroyed.
}
```

*The pool is created once; each request reuses a warm connection, so the costly handshake happens at startup instead of per request.*

```properties
# application.properties
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/shop
spring.datasource.username=app
spring.datasource.password=secret
spring.datasource.hikari.maximum-pool-size=10
spring.datasource.hikari.minimum-idle=5
spring.datasource.hikari.connection-timeout=30000
spring.datasource.hikari.idle-timeout=600000
```
```java
// Or in plain code without Spring:
HikariConfig cfg = new HikariConfig();
cfg.setJdbcUrl("jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/shop");
cfg.setUsername("app"); cfg.setPassword("secret");
cfg.setMaximumPoolSize(10);
DataSource ds = new HikariDataSource(cfg);
```

```java
// maximumPoolSize = 3; all 3 checked out and held:
Connection c1 = pool.getConnection();
Connection c2 = pool.getConnection();
Connection c3 = pool.getConnection();   // pool now empty

// a 4th concurrent caller blocks here, waiting up to connectionTimeout,
// then throws SQLTransientConnectionException if nothing is returned:
// Connection c4 = pool.getConnection();  // waits ~30s, then times out

c1.close();  // returning one unblocks a waiting caller
```

```java
try (Connection con = pool.getConnection();
     PreparedStatement ps = con.prepareStatement("UPDATE t SET x = 1")) {
    ps.executeUpdate();
}  // con returned to the pool even if executeUpdate throws -> no leak

// detect stragglers in config:
// spring.datasource.hikari.leak-detection-threshold=20000  (warn after 20s)
```

*try-with-resources guarantees the return; `leakDetectionThreshold` flags any leak you missed.*
